---
// Matches what the app does at the published version: 1.0.0 (PreCut 6b2636a), and from
// `aiRelease` the AI edit, translation, Pro, template link and B-roll parts (H below), from
// the app's cloud AI texts, docs/store/privacy-policy.md and docs/CLOUD_API.md.
import ProseLayout from "../layouts/ProseLayout.astro";
import { analytics, app, contactEmail, legalName, play } from "../config";
import { hasAccounts as A, hasAiRelease as H, legalDate } from "../lib/release";
import AccountSections from "../components/privacy/AccountSections.astro";
---

<ProseLayout
  title="Privacy policy"
  description={`What the ${app.name} app and this website do with your information.`}
  updated={legalDate}
>
  <p>
    {app.name} is a video editor for Android, made by {legalName}. This policy
    explains what the {app.name} app and this website do with your information.
  </p>
  {
    A ? (
      <p class="card">
        <strong>In short:</strong> you sign in with Google or your email, and{" "}
        {app.name} saves your projects to your account, with their videos,
        photos and other files, so they're on every phone you sign in to. Cloud
        AI features send only what they need, and only after you agree: the
        sound, to turn speech into words, or a written description of your
        project and your request, for AI edit and caption translation. There
        are no ads and no analytics in the app.
      </p>
    ) : H ? (
      <p class="card">
        <strong>In short:</strong> {app.name} edits on your phone, and your
        videos and photos never leave it. If you use a cloud AI feature and
        agree, that time, it sends only what that feature needs: the sound, to
        turn speech into words, or a written description of your project and
        your request, for AI edit and caption translation. There are no
        accounts, no ads and no analytics in the app.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p class="card">
        <strong>In short:</strong> {app.name} edits on your phone, and nothing
        leaves it unless you use a cloud AI feature and agree, that time, to
        send the sound. Then only the sound is sent, to turn speech into words.
        There are no accounts, no ads and no analytics.
      </p>
    )
  }

  {A && <AccountSections />}

  <h2>Cloud AI (optional, and asked every time)</h2>
  {
    H ? (
      <p>
        Some features can use cloud AI:{" "}
        <strong>Settings → Cloud AI → Check cloud AI</strong>, automatic
        captions, AI edit and caption translation. Before anything is sent,{" "}
        {app.name} shows what it is about to send and asks you. If you say no,
        nothing is sent. You can switch cloud AI off in{" "}
        <strong>Settings → Cloud AI</strong>.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>
        Some features can use cloud AI: today{" "}
        <strong>Settings → Cloud AI → Check cloud AI</strong>, and automatic
        captions when they arrive. Before anything is sent, {app.name} shows
        what it is about to send and asks you. If you say no, nothing is sent.
        You can switch cloud AI off in <strong>Settings → Cloud AI</strong>.
      </p>
    )
  }
  <p>When you agree:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>{H ? "What is sent for captions:" : "What is sent:"}</strong> only
      the sound you're making captions for (for Check cloud AI, a test phrase your phone
      speaks). Never the video, photos or project.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where it goes:</strong>
      {app.name}'s server, which runs on{" "}
      <a href="https://supabase.com/privacy">Supabase</a>
      in Ireland (EU). It passes the sound to ElevenLabs, a speech-to-text
      service, and sends the words and their timings back to your phone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What {app.name}'s server keeps:</strong> no audio and no words. It
      keeps a random ID that the app made for this installation (not linked to
      your name, email or phone number) and{" "}
      {
        H
          ? "how many minutes and AI edit requests it used this month, so each phone gets its free share."
          : "how many minutes it used this month, so each phone gets its free minutes."
      }{" "}
      Both are deleted after 13 months without use, or when you tap{" "}
      <strong>Settings → Cloud AI → Delete my cloud data</strong>. The server's
      logs record each request's time, type, size and outcome with a shortened
      form of that ID,{" "}
      {
        H
          ? "never the sound, the words, your requests or Claude's answers."
          : "never the sound or the words."
      }{" "}
      Like any web service, the hosting provider's logs also record your IP
      address. Logs are kept briefly to run and protect the service.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>ElevenLabs</strong> turns the sound into words for {app.name},
      under its own
      <a href="https://elevenlabs.io/privacy-policy">privacy policy</a> and data
      terms.
    </li>
    {
      H && (
        <>
          <li>
            <strong>For AI edit:</strong> what you ask for, and a written
            description of the project you're editing: how many clips there are,
            their lengths, order and settings (filters, speed, transitions), the
            words of your texts and captions, the names of your music and sound
            files, and what your phone worked out from the media itself: how
            loud each second is, where the picture changes, and a few words for
            what an on-phone image model sees in each shot (for example
            "seashore"). Never the video, photos or sound. {app.name}'s server
            passes them to <strong>Claude</strong>, an AI by{" "}
            <strong>Anthropic</strong>, which answers with changes for your
            phone to make; you review every change before it's kept.{" "}
            {app.name}'s server doesn't store the description, your request or
            Claude's answers; it keeps only how many AI edit requests this phone
            used this month, and their size and cost, with the random ID.
            Anthropic processes them for {app.name} under its commercial terms
            and <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/privacy">privacy
            policy</a>, which don't allow it to train its models on them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>For caption translation:</strong> the words of your captions
            and the language you chose, to {app.name}'s server and Claude, as
            for AI edit. Never the video or sound. A dub is spoken on your phone
            by its own text-to-speech voice.
          </li>
        </>
      )
    }
    <li>
      <strong>Google Play Integrity:</strong> before sending, {app.name} asks
      Google Play to confirm that it's the genuine {app.name} on a genuine
      Android phone, so no one else can use
      {app.name}'s minutes. Google Play makes that check under Google's terms,
      with information about the app and the device; {app.name} receives only
      the result.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Cloud AI needs the internet. Without a connection, {app.name} tells you and
    sends nothing.
  </p>

  {
    H && (
      <>
        <h2>{app.name} Pro (optional subscription)</h2>
        <p>
          {app.name} Pro is sold through Google Play, which handles the payment
          under Google's terms; {app.name} never sees your card or Google
          account details. To confirm a purchase, the app sends {app.name}'s
          server the purchase token Google Play gives it. The server checks it
          with Google and keeps the token, the plan, its state (active,
          cancelled, payment problem) and end date, linked to the random ID of
          each phone you use it on, so Pro works there. Google Play tells the
          server when the subscription renews, is cancelled or refunded. This is
          deleted after 13 months without use, or when you tap{" "}
          <strong>Settings → Cloud AI → Delete my cloud data</strong> (and no
          other phone of yours uses the purchase). Deleting it doesn't cancel
          the subscription; do that in Google Play.
        </p>

        <h2>Template links (optional)</h2>
        <p>
          When you share a template as a link, {app.name}'s server keeps the
          template (its layout, texts, effects and timing; never your photos,
          videos or music) under a short code, so anyone with the link can open
          it in {app.name} or on this website. It's kept until you tap{" "}
          <strong>Settings → Cloud AI → Delete my cloud data</strong>. Opening
          someone's link fetches their template and sends nothing about you.
        </p>

        <h2>Updates, ratings and installs</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Now and then {app.name} asks its server whether a newer version is
            out, so it can tell you. The request carries nothing about you; the
            server sees it like any web request.
          </li>
          <li>
            After a few good exports, {app.name} may let Google Play show its
            rating card. What you write there goes to Google Play under Google's
            terms. {app.name} only remembers, on your phone, when it last let
            the card show.
          </li>
          <li>
            If you install {app.name} from Google Play through a shared
            template's page, {app.name} reads Google Play's install referrer
            once, on your phone, to open that template on its first start.
            Nothing is sent.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </>
    )
  }

  <h2>What stays on your phone</h2>
  <p>To edit, {app.name} works with files on your device:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Photos and videos you pick.</strong> You choose them with
      Android's picker, and
      {app.name} can read only those. If the picker can't keep access to a file,{" "}
      {app.name} saves a copy in its own storage so your project still works
      later.
    </li>
    {
      H && (
        <>
          <li>
            <strong>Your gallery, for B-roll (optional).</strong> If you open
            B-roll, {app.name} asks to read your photos and videos (on Android
            14 and newer you can allow only some). It looks at the newest few
            hundred with an on-phone image model to find them by what's in
            them, and keeps that list of labels in its own storage. Nothing from
            your gallery is uploaded. You can take the access back in Android's
            settings at any time.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>AI on your phone.</strong> Background removal, auto
            reframe, noise reduction, follow person, finding moments for shorts
            and B-roll labels run on your phone with models built into the app.
            Nothing is sent for them.
          </li>
        </>
      )
    }
    <li>
      <strong>Microphone.</strong>
      {app.name} asks for the microphone only when you record a voiceover. The
      recording is saved in the app's storage as part of your project.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your projects.</strong>
      {app.name} saves drafts, covers, preview copies and other working files in
      its own storage on your phone.
      {A && " A copy of each project and its files is kept in your account (above)."}
      {
        H &&
          " So do your brand kit (a logo or fonts you add are copied in) and your answers to the welcome questions, which only change what it suggests first."
      }
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Exports.</strong> Videos, GIFs, audio and photos you export are
      saved to your phone's gallery or music folders, where other apps can see
      them like any other media.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Notifications.</strong>
      {app.name} shows a notification while an export runs, so you can follow or
      cancel it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Crash reports.</strong> If {app.name} closes unexpectedly, it
      saves a short report (the error, your phone model, Android and app
      versions, and {app.name}'s own recent log lines) in its own storage. It
      stays there unless you share it yourself from
      <strong>Settings → Crash reports</strong>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    {app.name} has {A ? "" : "no accounts, "}no ads and no analytics. When you share an
    export, Android's share sheet sends it to the app you choose. What happens
    next is up to that app.
  </p>

  <h2>Text to speech</h2>
  <p>
    If you use text to speech, {app.name} passes your text to the text-to-speech
    engine installed on your phone. That engine's own privacy policy applies to
    the text.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The website is hosted by{" "}
      <a href="https://www.netlify.com/privacy/">Netlify</a>. Like any web host,
      Netlify's servers record technical details of each visit, such as your IP
      address, browser and the page you asked for, to deliver and protect the
      site.
    </li>
    <li>
      The app download comes from
      <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub</a>,
      which records downloads in the same way.
    </li>
    {
      play.live && (
        <li>
          Links to Google Play carry which of this website's buttons you
          tapped (and, on a template's page, which template), so Google Play
          can count installs from each button and {app.name} can open that
          template after installing. They carry nothing about you.
        </li>
      )
    }
    <li>
      A shared template's page (an address starting /t/) asks {app.name}'s
      server for that template from your browser. The server's logs record the
      request like any other; nothing about you is kept with the template.
    </li>
    {
      analytics.plausibleScript ? (
        <li>
          The website sets no cookies. It counts visits and clicks on download
          buttons with{" "}
          <a href="https://plausible.io/data-policy">Plausible Analytics</a>,
          which collects no personal data and doesn't track you across sites.
          The download pages check whether you're on Android, an iPhone or a
          computer to show the right instructions; that check happens in your
          browser and nothing is sent.
        </li>
      ) : (
        <li>
          The website sets no cookies and uses no analytics or trackers. The
          download pages check whether you're on Android, an iPhone or a
          computer to show the right instructions; that check happens in your
          browser and nothing is sent.
        </li>
      )
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
  <ul>
    {
      A && (
        <li>
          <strong>Settings → Account → Delete account</strong> deletes your
          account and, at once, every project and file saved to it, your
          sign-in details and the link to your Pro purchases. You can also ask
          on the <a href="/delete-data">deletion page</a>. Deleting the account
          doesn't cancel a Pro subscription (do that in Google Play).
        </li>
      )
    }
    <li>
      Deleting a project removes its files from the app.
      <strong>Settings → Clear preview cache</strong> frees working copies.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Settings → Cloud AI → Delete my cloud data</strong>{" "}
      {
        H
          ? `deletes the random ID and usage ${app.name}'s server keeps for this phone, its Pro purchase records (unless another phone of yours uses them) and the templates you shared as links.`
          : `deletes the random ID and minutes ${app.name}'s server keeps for this phone.`
      }
    </li>
    <li>
      Uninstalling {app.name} removes everything it stored on your phone, except
      media you exported to your gallery. What {app.name}'s server keeps is then
      deleted after 13 months without use.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    More detail: <a href="/delete-data">Delete your data</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    {app.name} isn't directed at children
    {A ? " under 13, and accounts are for people aged 13 and over." : "."}{" "}
    {
      H
        ? "Cloud AI sends only what the person using the app chooses to send."
        : "Cloud AI sends only sound that the person using the app chooses to send."
    }
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the new version will be posted on this page with a
    new date.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions or requests about your information:{" "}
    <a href={`mailto:${contactEmail}`}>{contactEmail}</a>.
  </p>
</ProseLayout>
